<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karlag</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1931">1931</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Karaganda Corrective Labour Camp</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karlag, the Karaganda Corrective Labour Camp, was one of the largest camps of the Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>, on the steppe of central Kazakhstan. From 1931 to 1959 its prisoners worked mainly on vast farms supplying the Karaganda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>-mining region. Hundreds of thousands passed through it, including political prisoners and deported people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karlag.jpg" alt="Karlag" width="316" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: File:Gulag Location Map.svg · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karlag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
